From: szabo@techbook.com (Nick Szabo)
Subject: DIET: A & C

Perry Metzger: 
> If anyone has some information on sources of solid data on optimal
> dosages of these and other similar substances, I'd heartily appreciate
> hearing it.

Unlike most pharmaceuticals targeted at specific, obvious 
disease symptoms, vitamins & minerals (the way life extensionists 
use them) are targeted at a wide range of subtle metabolic 
processes, eg free radical scavenging.  The dosages commonly 
used are based on rules of thumb, which are in turn based 
on statistical studies, studies of how cultured cells and 
lab animals react to the substance, blood levels over
time, and other indirect or partial clues to what is going on.
Different effects have different optimal dosages; that's why
U.S. RDAs, designed to prevent obvious disease, are much lower
than most life extension doages, why life extension dosages differ
from dosages for other purposes, and in general why there are so
many contradictory recommendations.

Recommended dosages are typically well below levels shown to be harmful.  
This window is orders of magnitude wide for some substances (Vit. E, C, 
beta-carotene, most B vitamins) and narrow for some others (Vit. A, D, 
most minerals).  

Truly optimum dosage will vary from period to period and person to
person.  I have been taking 400 mcg of chromium picolinate a
day to help with weight loss, since that was what was used in
the weight-loss study I read and other books consider a safe
level; but now that I'm down I'm switching
to 200 mcg/day.  I take more antioxidants if I've been doing
or know I'm about to do strenuous exercise.   I mostly steer
clear of naked amino acids on empty stomachs, there's just too 
much vague neurotoxicity data out there for my tastes.  

Like medicine itself, this stuff is not physics where a bit of
mathematics will match reality to high precision.  It's not
straightforward chemistry either.  It's mostly a matter of
knowledge and common sense.  Unless you take the time to
learn quite a bit in the field, I'd recommend seeing a good
nutritionist knowledgeable about life extension, or being 
conservative, especially when it comes to minerals and amino acids.  

As for the original studies, Medline is by far the best source.
There are conference proceedings published as books (eg
the _Third Conference on Vitamin C_, 1987, Burns et. al. ed).
Also check out the references in Pearson & Shaw's books, although
that's stuff's getting a bit stale now.   There's 
a potentially endless amount of homework you can do, but
even after that common sense must still play a major role.

