On Wed, 9 Jun 1993, Mike Wiik wrote:

> [ on dairy-munching mutation ]
> 
> > Well, possibly longer. All I know is that the mutation isn't present
> > in a large fraction of the world's population. Cheese isn't very big
> > in, say, China.
> > 
> > Perry
> 
> Indeed, I've heard that milk products are considered fairly disgusting
> in China. I've read somewhere (probably in a Marvin Harris book) that
> dairy foods were an adaptation to provide vitamin D in the diet where
> sunlight wasn't plentiful year-round...

You have to be a little careful of Harris.  He's a great storyteller,
but not always real concerned about empirical testing of his stories.
On this one he's mostly right, but if you're interested you should
get the story from Durham (_Coevolution ..._), who actually proves it, and
also points out the essential memetic part of the story (which is a line
of explanation that Harris makes a point of avoiding).  Lactose
absorbtion evolved in latitudes where sunlight was insufficient to
maintain adequate vitamin D levels, but only among populations that
already had dairying as part of their cultural adaptation (i.e were
using processed milk products like yogurt or cheese).  So Eskimos and
subarctic Indians, for example, are lactose intolerant just like most 
of the rest of the world.  Durham also argues that among the lactose-digesting
populations of Europe you find mythological reflections of the dairying
lifestyle--i.e. while in all Indo-European mythology bovines play a large
role, it's only in the northern European milk drinking cultures that
the mythology presents them explicitly as milk-producing rather than
as symbols of (masculine) fertility.


