Date: Fri, 11 Jun 1993 13:31:26 -0700 (PDT)
From: szabo@techbook.com (Nick Szabo)
Subject: SPACE/FREE: Governments in Space

One straightfoward way for a space colony (a truly self
sufficient kind, with ample supplies of volatiles, metals, etc.
and a very flexible, diverse industrial base) to protect itself is 
to put a lot of mass between itself and vacuum.  A few tens of
meters of asteroidal steel should protect against any but the
largest fusion devices.   (I'd want that much to protect against
meteors anyway, especially in the asteroid belt).  

The space colony itself could be made stealthy -- put Earthside
communications arrays and spaceship ports well off-site, paint the 
colony with dark tar, hide it amongst a bunch of similar-looking asteroids 
or large metallized-film balloon dummies (Macy's parade in the asteroid
belt!), etc.  Earthsiders might not even know where the colony
is, within several trillion cubic klicks of space.  In fact the
space colony need not be located in one place at all; it might
consist of a large constellation of habitats, industrial sites,
spaceports, etc., economically dependent on each other, but
potentially independent of Earth.  Transporting cargo across
a million klicks of space in the same orbit is trivially cheap.

For larger warheads -- multi-gigaton fusion or antimatter
devices, diverted asteroids, and the like -- the settlers
have to detect them well ahead of time and divert them, perhaps with
their own large, crude fusion devices.  They may have a large  perimeter
constellation surrounding the space habitat(s) at a few 100,000 klicks,
consisting of active and passive detectors, fusion-warhead interceptors, 
etc.   

Another way to attack the colony might be a spoof payload, eg hide 
large fusion devices in a shipment of ice.  Another good reason to put 
the interplanetary spaceship port well off-site. 

On the flip side, assuming we have the tech to build such space
colonies in the first place, it will be trivially cheap for
the space colonists to wipe out any city on Earth, just by
putting gigatons of steel on collision course with Earth.  Space 
colonies might also take up the habit of trying to wipe out each other,
if they were so inclined.  If such an environment emerged, it
would be much like Medieval Europe, with most of the resources
going into build huge nickel-iron castles.   Hopefully space 
civilization will be of a more cooperative nature.

