Date: Fri, 11 Jun 93 13:43:14 -0700
From: tcmay@netcom.com (Timothy C. May)
Subject: Nanotech Moonshots and Government Funding

Someone whose views I respect enormously has sent me e-mail urging me to
not make charges here on this list that Eric Drexler cannot defend himself
against, that I should instead offer to debate Eric in the pages of The
Foresight Update.

I disagree for several reasons, which I communicated back to my colleague,
but which needs to be shared here:

1. I have made no formal charges, simply given my opinion that over the
past few years Eric has been leaning more and more toward pushing for a
national effort of some sort, for whatever reasons. His happiness at
reaching Al Gore, and a Vice Admiral at the Pentagon (Adm. Jeremiah, I
recall), and in talking to others in Washington, is some evidence.

2. In the first few hours of 1991, at a New Year's Eve Party at the late
Phil Salin's house, Eric and I argued for nearly 3 hours these issues, with
Eric taking the view that nanotech is the most important technology there
is, and hence funding of it is necessary. He cited the growing Japanese
national efforts. Both Phil and I pressed on him whether he was calling for
national funding, a national effort (the "Moonshot" approach), or similar.
We skirted the issue and said it "might" be needed, and it might be a case
where the benefits to the populace justified a centralized approach.

Discussions at other times have seen similar results. Even Mark Miller,
whom I also respect enormously, has suggested that a national research
project may be the only way to fund nanotech. 

(At the Hackers Conference last fall, which I referred to in another
posting, Eric cited the $25 billion dollar cost of the Apollo program and
said that with $25 billion (in today's dollars, no less) the research
needed to build the primitive assemblers described in "Unbounding the
Future" could very likely be done. He asked the audience which result
they'd rather have: moon rocks or general assemblers? He got the
predictable response (though I think the reasoning is wrong, for a lot of
reasons). This sure sounded to me like an appeal for funding at the
national level.)

3. I don't have any idea what the Foresight Institute is arguing for in the
way of funding...I no longer get the "Update," and sci.nanotech hasn't
carried all the issues. I will agree with my e-mail colleague that Eric has
*not* lobbied for a NASA-type program, per se, at least not in so many
words, and that his approach is more subtle. But there is little doubt in
my mind that Eric would like to see a national research and development
effort. He would probably _rather_ have the money come from private
sources, being a free-market libertarian, sort, but if it has to come from
the government, so be it. (One time in perhaps understandable frustration,
he turned to me and said, "So why doesn't Intel fund my work!?")

4. Why not argue this in the pages of "The Foresight Update"? First of all,
I'm on *this* list, and *this* is where the topic has come up. Frankly, I
wish Eric would join our list (in my opinion, by not being active on the
Net, or in groups like this, he is too insular, too cut-off from
contrasting views. On the other hand, he may get more done.) Second, these
issues of Big Science vs. Private Development go beyond just nanotech,
e.g., to space development, and so are not necessarily better off in
another forum. Third, I am fairly sure negative debate in Eric's journal
would not be viewed kindly. Eric is undoubtedly messianic about nanotech
(can anyone seriously doubt this? I would probably be so, as well, had
invented it and lived it for 15 years!). I doubt a debate would help, or
would be feasible.

(I'm willing to debate anyone on *this* list, though not via the
time-consuming formalisms some have suggested. A series of posts and
counterposts, as we usually see, is what I mean by a debate.)

These are the reasons I will continue to express my views here. I continue
to hold Drexler in high esteem, and I even will if and when he takes a more
public role in any national effort (which given the funding problems these
days, will likely *not* be pushed by either Al Gore or Admiral Jeremiah).

But I think he is mistaken about the needed pace for nanotechnology and is
too impatient to allow natural market forces to set the development pace.

As Krzys' notes, a national effort will actually _slow down_ the real
progress, the sustainable progress. As I have noted so many times before, a
huge effort could have been launched as early as 1960 to build a "computer
on a chip," and for some tens fo billions of taxpayer dollars, it might
have been built. But the supporting technologies, the learning curves of so
many earlier products, the chance to learn from dead-end mistakes, would
not have occured....the "computer on a chip" would be in the Smithsonian
and people would be debating whether we should build another one!

Slight overstatement, but not by much.

Cheers!

