From: price@price.demon.co.uk (Michael Clive Price)
Subject: QM: Theories vs. Interpretations

Many-worlds _does_ make testable predictions.

Dr David Deutsch has proposed an experiment by which a conscious,
artifical mind, composed of reversable logic circuits and memory units
can perform a simple experiment (say, measuring the spin of an electron)
and storing the result, in its reversible memory.  It then reverses the
memory storage process and interaction with the test electron.  If many-
worlds is correct the original state of the electron is restored.  What
does copenhagen/HUT/implicate order predict?  Depends who you ask.... 

This experiment will be constructable within decades, according to
Deutsch.  Thus many-worlds has the status general relativity had in
1915.  Experiments have been proposed, but it will take a few
years/decades before they can be conducted.

Incidently the restoration of the electron's original state is an
example of the merging of 'independent' worlds that Robin and Fnerd
mentioned.  I would still describe them as 'independent' because there
is no experiment than can be conducted in one of the worlds, and not the
other, to reveal the other's presence or absence.  Any such experiment
would prevent the merger of the worlds.  Worlds merge when things are
forgotten (when information is lost).  Worlds split when things are
discovered (when information is gained).  Sounds like loose talk, but
it has a precise mathematical meaning.

