Ugh, this is really a very complex issue.  I think I can address this 
superficially, but an in depth answer to your question requires a lot more
than one (or a dozen) e-mail messages.  Not to argue from authority, but
to establish my point of view, I will mention that my former position at
the Rand Corporation was as a senior analyst on a project entitled "Command
and Control Integration of Strategic Space Defense Assets".  Part of my
purview was to understand current and developing technology in target
discrimination.  As always in the defense biz, the capability to defend
is strongly enhanced by intelligence.  For example, if one knows that the
attacker will be fielding a swarm of targets, some decoys and some warheads
"only a millimeter across", then one can optimize a defense system using
low power rapid firing weaponry, whereas if one expects fewer massive targets,
then high powered beam weapons or kinetic energy weapons are more appropriate.
If one has no clue what the attack capability is, then one must cover
as many possibilities as one can.


That having been said, I believe that current target discrimination technology,
including, but not limited to phased array radar is sufficient to discriminate
any exoatmospheric target with noticable destructive capability.  Perry's
claim that "you could track everything within  several light seconds" seems
reasonable to me, if "everything" is restricted to devices which destroy by
explosion (grey goo warheads may be more difficult).  This is not to say that
there will not be an actual limit to the number of things which can be
actively tracked by any given system, only that one could build a system
which could track any given number of things.

This being the case, then in order to successfully attack a space-based colony
you would have to either overwhelm the target acquisition system, overwhelm 
the active defense (more attacking devices than could be actively destroyed
by available weaponry), or use attack devices that could not be descriminated
by the defense sensor system, or by subterfuge (e.g. a planet-buster concealed
in a commercial vessel before the outset of active hostilites, essentially
the terrorist approach). 


In the final analysis, the capability to successfully attack or successfully
defend depends on the resources available to both parties.  Assets in space
are more vulnerable than assets within the atmosphere, but that is
a disadvantage to the colony (more easily destroyed) in addition to being
an asset (can more easily destroy attacking devices).  There are far too
many unstated assumptions to give a definitive answer, so I will end
with the handwaving answer that given current defense and offense technologies,
it would be possible to defend a colony far enough from the earth (say no
closer than the moon).  It would, however, cost significantly more to 
provide the defense than it would to generate an attack, so the cost advantage
rests with the attacker, as is the case with ballistic missile defense
on earth today.  So while it is technologically possible to provide a defense,
it will likely not be economically feasible for any colony to provide
such a defense.  



